Commentary of al-Biqa'i (Nazm al-Durar) on surah 14, verse 50:

Then he clarified their clothing by his saying: ﴿Their garments﴾, i.e., their closely fitting shirts, ﴿of tar﴾, which is that with which camels are daubed against mange, and it is among its qualities that fire spreads quickly through it, and it is black in color and foul in smell. (p-440)

Since this garment, along with its stench and repulsiveness, is highly receptive to fire, he clarified that He would set fire upon them, so He said: ﴿And the fire covers﴾. Since the face is the noblest part of the animal, humiliating it is a great humiliation to its owner, so He mentioned it and placed it first, to hasten the conveying of the humiliation, and said: ﴿their faces, the fire﴾, i.e., it overspreads them with its blazing. Thus it is known that its covering them necessarily entails its kindling in what is smeared with tar, a fortiori (min bab al-ula);
